al stuff but I'm not  going to worry about that because that's  not really what I'm when I'm interested  in I think that one sense of eclectic  that doesn't really apply to me but a  lot of people have in mind when they  they say that word or when they you know  are describing themselves or talking  about other people is somebody who just  sort of picks and chooses you know at  whim or at random whatever it is that  they like so you know you ask somebody  what kind of music are you into and they  say I have a collective taste and that  means that they sort of pick and choose  a little bit of everything I'm not  really like that in philosophy as a  matter of fact I don't even really try  at this point to like do a  representative sample you know I need  something from you know 19th century  German philosophy and I need something  from 19th century French philosophy and  something from 20th century  anglo-american analytic and some  classical American I don't do it that  way right I'm not a a person who picks  and chooses sort of on a quota system  anything like that although actually  when I when I do teach classes I try to  give a good you know representative  sample of what there is out there  there was actually a movement in in  philosophy that I should mention as well  that was called eclecticism and  associated with Victor cuisine and other  intellectuals and and they were they  were doing something a little bit closer  to what it is that I mean by eclecticism  but you know when I think of somebody  who's an eclectic one of the key  thinkers that has really grown on me  over the years and who I kind of looked  to as a model is Cicero if you ever read  Cicero's dialogues and I'm gonna shoot  one of these videos specifically about  why it is that I love Cicero so much you  know Cicero has it's almost like he's  telling a joke right an epicurean a  skeptic and a stoic walking to a bar  except in this case they're walking into  Cicero's house or they're hanging out  and they're gonna talk about you know  the nature of the gods or the way a  republic should be set up or what is the  the final good and and the worst evil  for human beings and they're you know  he's gonna set them up to talk with each  other and to present their positions and  to you know criticize other positions on  the way in very very structured  sometimes very erudite ways and then  they're gonna critique each other so  it's it's a really a genuine dialogue I  love Cicero's dialogical works and even  in his less dialogical works where he's  just presenting you know some some  theories he'll he'll draw from a number  of different thinkers and schools and  that's part of what I really like about  about that approach that eclectic  approach you got to know the  philosophical sources inside and out you  take what you find valuable from them  you perhaps strip off the elements that  you don't find valuable you have some  reason why you're doing that and that is  the approach that that I myself take in  philosophy so I probably should say  something about you know who I upon and  I'll do that very briefly here and then  I'll tell you a little bit more about it  in a bit and why I'm particularly  attracted to them so I think a lot of  people know me these days in part  because of my connections with the  modern stoic movement and being editor  of stoicism today I really do draw quite  heavily on stoicism stoicism is  something that I began researching 20  years ago and I'd say in the last 10  years I've really gotten into it in a  serious way I see it as a pretty  valuable set of conversation partners  but I'm not actually a stoic in an  Orthodox sense you know there's there's  some things about stoic philosophy that  I I don't you know completely accept and  so I don't actually call myself a stoic  although I'm fine if other people want  to tell me that I  I am similarly I draw very strongly on  the Aristotelian tradition but I'm not  really an Aristotelian although I'm you  know very Aristotelian in certain  respects I draw up on the plate in this  tradition which doesn't just include  Plato himself but also middle Platonism  with people like Plutarch Wirth's being  systematized some Neoplatonic thinkers  some of them pagans some of them I like  fuel of Alexandria I like I like some of  the Christian neoplatonist s'right  there's there's a lot of Christian  thinkers who I find quite interesting  sometimes precisely because of what  they're doing when said synthesizing  Christianity and Greek philosophy  sometimes in spite of that and you know  that takes me through the Middle Ages  you know I read and draw quite a bit  John Cassian an Augustine of Clement of  Alexandria Anselm of Canterbury Thomas  Aquinas but I don't really I still don't  belong to to any of them in the sense of  being an East right and then there's  many modern thinkers who I particularly  like there's a lot of things I really  enjoy and des cartes philosophy although  I'm not a Cartesian there's a lot of  things I think are really cool about  Leibniz and about Hume and even about  count you know I think a lot of people  know me of course because of the half  hour Hegel series and I do use Hegel I  think he's right about some things I  don't buy the overall scheme and I'm not  really think Eliot in that sense  Kierkegaard marx nietzsche you know  these are all thinkers who I really  enjoy some some things from and and draw  upon but I'm not  a member of their school so you know if  you if you wanted to say it very broadly  you could say that you know in in my my  thinking in my practice as a  philosophical counselor as a you know  consultant I draw up on the plate inist  the Aristotelian and the stoic  traditions even the sometimes the  Epicureans or the skeptics you know a  lot of Christian philosophy some of  which is much more Augustinian some of  which is more Aristotelian based you  know like an Intimus the cart you know  hobbes  hume live nets in pascal of course very  important thinker for me some of the the  the other more contemporary approaches  you know dialectical we might call it in  a certain respect deconstructive there's  some things about that I like I like the  psychoanalytic tradition or these parts  of it right  particularly Jacques Lacan I'm  interested in and Hans Cole and then  some of the the other people and you  know we can go on and on and on of the  existentialist right although they're a  mixed bag too so I draw upon all of this  stuff and you know I'm not saying that I  have like a comprehensive grasp of the  history of thought or anything like that  but I do have a pretty broad background  and I can find a lot of things that I  deem interesting or useful and like to  bring together and do comparative work  with and I think that's part of what it  means to be a philosophical eclectic it  also means that as opposed to somebody  who you know is in a particular movement  and identifies that that tradition my  work is really really really never done  there's always more to read there's  always more to think about I don't  really have a a permanent home you might  say although I'm welcome in many places  and and happily so unless you know  somebody wants to kick me out for not  being properly Orthodox  so you know what I see the the task for  myself is these days a lot of is what I  do here on YouTube there's so many  wonderful interesting thinkers whose  thoughts that I find valuable and I want  to share them with other people and then  you know when I get into two particular  counseling situations you know thinking  about particular issues then I start you  know applying this one this one this one  this one and a lot of that actually  depends on what it is that the people  that I'm talking with want to focus on  right  not everybody is into this philosopher  who I'm into so maybe I use a different  language a different thinker to reach  them and I guess the last thing that I  should talk about since you know this is  not just about philosophical commitments  but but also development how did I end  up here you know I would say that in  many respects for the good portion of my  early philosophical career I was  struggling with with two competing  desires and one of them was to get to  know as much of the the valuable  philosophy whether it's you know what we  call the history of philosophy or  whether it was contemporary stuff to get  to know as much of that stuff as I could  I mean I was just you know eating it up  and and going through as much as I could  possibly take in and loving it on the  other hand I did want to have a place  where I could I could be a tradition in  which I could locate myself a community  to which I could see myself as belonging  and very often those two kind of  conflict with each other because to be  part of a community often means  accepting a certain we might call it  party line about other philosophers and  what took place in the history of  philosophy and I never really found  a single narrative that I could really  totally buy into because I you know if  somebody would say well Aristotle did  this and this or the Christian thinkers  in the Middle Ages did this and this or  you know those pulse modernists are  doing this and this I would actually  read the figures and I'd be like wow  part of that might be true but really  that's kind of a distortion and you know  if you've read this you wouldn't say  that sort of thing wow I did read it and  well I guess you didn't really read it  carefully you kind of put the blinders  on at the start and I got a lot of  experiences like that and and so  eventually I had to get okay with being  an eclectic and I suppose what I'll  close on is something I brought up in in  some other videos so you know like I  mentioned you know people think that I'm  coming from this point of view or that  point of view or this point of view or  that point of view as if I'm you know  that's my my fundamental commitment  because I like to do justice to to the  ideas and in particular thinkers works  and I like to present them in a way that  that's coherent and is somewhat  plausible at least and so I have a lot  of people thinking that I belong to one  school or another when I when I actually  don't but I am you know very happy to be  engaged in conversation so you know when  I go to like here at Marquette every  year just down the street they have a  nice Aristotelian conference well then I  talk about Aristotle a lot and other  people in the Aristotelian tradition at  snowicon' I'm gonna talk mostly about  stoicism you know if I'm at the st.  Anselm conference probably I'll talk a  lot about st. Anselm and they're gonna  talk about Thomas Aquinas do it and some  other thinkers and so it makes perfect  sense to me that that's how I would live  my my life and make use of the remaining  years that are left to me to study and  teach and do some scholarship and you  know do some presentations and  application of philosophy so you know  I've come to be comfortable didn't it  didn't happen right away but I've come  to be comfortable with being an eclectic  with you know seeing that's where I'll  probably be for a long time I don't know  I might end up you know departing from  that and saying I'm definitely going for  this school or definitely going for that  school you never know right but that  doesn't look like what's in the cards  for me and again I don't have any  problem with other people  wholeheartedly you know saying my my you  know mind my heart my spirit belongs to  so-and-so right that's great  that's just not not where I'm going and  it makes the the dialogue the ongoing  conversation that is philosophy a lot  more fun if we do have people who are  genuinely hardcore committed I think so  that's enough about this topic be  interested to see what your comments are  and hope that this fills you in those of  you that have been asking me for these  these more personal videos about me and  and what it is that I I think and how I  do philosophy  you
 

